ents the companion side of the pairing contract: generate/store the device key in Keychain, encode the QR payload, sign the canonical message, submit the pending request, poll with the poll token, and store the completed session token in Keychain. This session intentionally does not modify that sibling project or deploy this server/display work.</p><h2 id="current-client-boundary">Current client boundary</h2><p>The separate Apple client project is <code>../now-playing-ios</code> in the workspace. It contains the shared Swift catalog/session/media models, bearer API client, Keychain pairing, adaptive iPhone/iPad shell, canonical device Live Queue, AVFoundation playback, Application Support cache, attached-SSD resolver, native playback-history reporter, Siri/App Intents, and the standard CarPlay template coordinator. See the native repository&#x27;s <code>docs/native-feature-surfaces.md</code> for the user-facing feature matrix.</p><h3 id="current-server-contract">Current Server contract</h3><p>The current Server remains authoritative for the existing opaque <code>MobileTrack</code> identities, metadata, playlists, artwork, Home moOde/Alexa queue state, and history qualification. A native device queue may hold those identities and play authorized local/server media, but the Server still owns the shared mobile contracts and the history/scrobble decision. The client never receives raw MPD/file paths, Track Keys, Qobuz credentials, or Alexa credentials.</p><p>The concise contract checklist for the separate native-client audit is the <a href="https://github.com/teacherguy2020/now-playing/blob/main/docs/ios/server-to-ios-contract-handoff-2026-10.md">Server → iOS contract handoff</a>.</p><p>The two contracts that connect local native playback back to the Server are:</p><ul><li><code>GET /v1/mobile/local-source/manifest</code>, which reports canonical-ID to</li></ul><p>relative-file availability; a missing or stale local copy is a cache miss, not a new track identity; and</p><ul><li><code>POST /v1/mobile/playback/events</code>, which accepts canonical progress events</li></ul><p>and lets the Server qualify history and Last.fm submission.</p><h3 id="approved-target-not-current-behavior">Approved target, not current behavior</h3><p>The approved future architecture is an iOS-device-owned canonical Catalog that aggregates provider observations and can support server-independent native playback. That target does <strong>not</strong> yet define or implement identity merging, metadata-conflict resolution, provider synchronization, or the final server-independent playback contract. This page must not turn those open design questions into a claimed current API.</p><h2 id="deployment-and-verification-status">Deployment and verification status</h2><p>The server/display pairing flow and Tailscale transport were deployed and verified on the live installation on 2026-09-27. The completed transport checks were:</p><ol><li>Tailscale was installed and authenticated on the Sonuvi host.</li><li>The host received a stable tailnet identity and MagicDNS name.</li><li>Tailscale Serve was configured as a tailnet-only HTTPS proxy to local API</li></ol><p>port <code>3101</code>; Funnel remains disabled.</p><ol><li>Tailscale issued a trusted certificate for the MagicDNS hostname.</li><li><code>MOBILE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL</code> was changed to that HTTPS hostname and the Sonuvi</li></ol><p>service was restarted successfully.</p><ol><li>The pairing flow was tested from the Config and Controller Settings</li></ol><p>displays, and the native app completed pairing and catalog browsing.</p><p>Remaining native-client verification and hardening:</p><ol><li>With Tailscale connected, confirm the controller-profile URL above from</li></ol><p>the iPhone and iPad.</p><ol><li>Generate a fresh QR and re-pair any client that still has the old LAN URL.</li><li>Authorize a known MP3 and verify <code>206</code> range behavior from the tailnet.</li><li>Authorize one non-MP3 file and verify bounded transcoding/cache behavior.</li><li>Test the same media URL from AVFoundation while the phone is locked.</li><li>Confirm remote playback leaves the home MPD queue, Alexa playback, and</li></ol><p>moOde configuration unchanged.</p><ol><li>With an attached SSD, verify the cache-miss matrix: disconnected source,</li></ol><p>absent canonical track, stale size entry, and reconnect. Each case must preserve the canonical track ID and fall back to remote media when needed.</p><ol><li>Exercise all thirteen Home shelf sources and their actions, target handoff, Siri collection</li></ol><p>intents, and the playback-diagnostics toggle on unlocked devices.</p><ol><li>For pairing, retain coverage for the QR payload, public-key proof, display</li></ol><p>approval, wrong code, replay/expiry, restart invalidation, and confirmation that the display never receives the session token.</p><p>Full SSD synchronization, server-side refresh-token revocation, remaining Siri/Alexa physical acceptance, and CarPlay vehicle validation remain separate follow-ups. The CarPlay Audio entitlement itself is approved and configured in the signed native target.</p><h2 id="timestamp">Timestamp</h2><p>Last updated: 2026-10-20 18:54 America/Chicago</p></article><script>(function(){var p=(location.pathname.split("/").pop()||"README.html");document.querySelectorAll("nav a[href]").forEach(function(a){if(a.getAttribute("href")===p){a.classList.add("nav-current");var d=a.closest("details");if(d)d.open=true;}});})();</script><script>(function(){var i=document.getElementById("nav-search");if(!i)return;})();</script></body></html>
