+}</code></pre><h2 id="explicit-canonical-vibe-seed">Explicit canonical Vibe seed</h2><p>The bearer mobile Queue Wizard adapter accepts an explicit canonical catalog seed without exposing the web/admin Vibe route:</p><pre><code>POST /v1/mobile/queue-wizard/vibe
+Authorization: Bearer &lt;mobile-session-token&gt;
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{&quot;trackId&quot;:&quot;trk_opaque_id&quot;,&quot;targetQueue&quot;:12,&quot;playNow&quot;:true,&quot;keepPlaying&quot;:true}</code></pre><p><code>trackId</code> is resolved against the current Server catalog. The Server derives the artist/title seed and invokes the existing Queue Wizard seeded builder, preserving its queue-size, play-now, and keep-playing semantics. The accepted response is sanitized:</p><pre><code>{&quot;ok&quot;:true,&quot;accepted&quot;:true,&quot;jobId&quot;:&quot;vibe_job_opaque&quot;,&quot;trackId&quot;:&quot;trk_opaque_id&quot;,&quot;targetQueue&quot;:12}</code></pre><p><code>targetQueue</code> must be an integer from 1 through 200. The client cannot submit artist/title text, a file path, an MPD ID, a renderer URL, or a Track Key. The no-<code>trackId</code> form remains as a compatibility adapter for older mobile callers that seed from the current Home track; new native migration work must use the canonical-ID form.</p><h2 id="native-device-vibe-preview">Native-device Vibe preview</h2><p>Native-device Vibe recommendations use a separate non-mutating contract. This is not the Home Queue Wizard job endpoint above:</p><pre><code>POST /v1/mobile/vibe/preview
+Authorization: Bearer &lt;mobile-session-token&gt;
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{&quot;trackId&quot;:&quot;trk_opaque_id&quot;,&quot;targetQueue&quot;:12}</code></pre><p>The Server resolves the canonical <code>trackId</code>, derives the seed internally, and reuses the existing Vibe engine&#x27;s dry-run path. The successful response is <code>200</code> and contains only canonical mobile track identities and safe metadata:</p><pre><code>{
+  &quot;ok&quot;: true,
+  &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;native-device-vibe-preview&quot;,
+  &quot;trackId&quot;: &quot;trk_opaque_id&quot;,
+  &quot;targetQueue&quot;: 12,
+  &quot;count&quot;: 2,
+  &quot;unresolvedCount&quot;: 0,
+  &quot;tracks&quot;: [
+    {&quot;id&quot;:&quot;trk_recommendation_1&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Example&quot;,&quot;artist&quot;:&quot;Example Artist&quot;},
+    {&quot;id&quot;:&quot;trk_recommendation_2&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Another&quot;,&quot;artist&quot;:&quot;Another Artist&quot;}
+  ]
+}</code></pre><p>Recommendation rows are mapped to the canonical catalog by their exact server-side library-file identity. The endpoint never falls back to an ambiguous title/artist match and never returns the file path. Unmapped rows are omitted and counted in <code>unresolvedCount</code>; if no returned row resolves to a canonical track, the response is <code>422</code> with <code>MOBILE_VIBE_PREVIEW_UNRESOLVED</code>. Invalid fields or bounds return <code>400</code> <code>MOBILE_VIBE_PREVIEW_INVALID_REQUEST</code>, an unknown seed returns <code>404</code> <code>MOBILE_TRACK_NOT_FOUND</code>, an ineligible stream/track returns <code>400</code> <code>MOBILE_VIBE_INELIGIBLE_TRACK</code>, and an engine timeout returns <code>504</code> <code>MOBILE_VIBE_PREVIEW_TIMEOUT</code>. Other engine failures return sanitized <code>502</code> <code>MOBILE_VIBE_PREVIEW_FAILED</code>.</p><p>This preview does not create a Queue Wizard job, mutate the Home MPD queue, start Home playback, alter saved queues or physical shuffle state, release or take over Qobuz Connect, or take over UPnP. The mobile caller never sends a Track Key; the internal dry-run bridge remains server-only. <code>catalogBuiltAt</code> is the current catalog timestamp or <code>null</code>. Each <code>tracks</code> entry uses the existing safe mobile-track shape (<code>id</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>artist</code>, <code>albumArtist</code>, <code>album</code>, <code>albumId</code>, <code>artistId</code>, <code>trackNumber</code>, <code>genre</code>, <code>durationSec</code>, <code>format</code>, <code>musicBrainz</code>, <code>availability</code>, and bearer-protected <code>artworkUrl</code>, with optional <code>sourceAudio</code>).</p><h2 id="next-up-parity">Next Up parity</h2><p>The native iPhone and CarPlay surfaces use <code>/v1/mobile/next-up</code> for the single compact successor shown beside the player. The iPad uses the same target-aware contract in a summary shelf at the top of Home, alongside queue