+{&quot;action&quot;:&quot;repeat&quot;}
+{&quot;action&quot;:&quot;consume&quot;}
+{&quot;action&quot;:&quot;consume&quot;,&quot;state&quot;:&quot;on&quot;}
+{&quot;action&quot;:&quot;consume&quot;,&quot;state&quot;:&quot;off&quot;}
+{&quot;action&quot;:&quot;repeatMode&quot;,&quot;mode&quot;:&quot;off&quot;}
+{&quot;action&quot;:&quot;repeatMode&quot;,&quot;mode&quot;:&quot;all&quot;}
+{&quot;action&quot;:&quot;repeatMode&quot;,&quot;mode&quot;:&quot;one&quot;}
+{&quot;action&quot;:&quot;seekRelative&quot;,&quot;seconds&quot;:-30}
+{&quot;action&quot;:&quot;seekPercent&quot;,&quot;percent&quot;:50}</code></pre><p><code>seekRelative.seconds</code> must be a non-zero integer from <code>-600</code> through <code>600</code>. <code>seekPercent.percent</code> must be a finite number from <code>0</code> through <code>100</code>; the Server rounds it to the whole percentage accepted by the existing playback authority. Unknown fields, raw MPD paths, renderer URLs, and Track-Key-only requests are rejected. <code>previous</code> is translated to the existing server-side <code>prev</code> action, and <code>repeat</code> toggles the existing Home repeat mode. <code>consume</code> without a <code>state</code> preserves the existing blind Consume toggle; when supplied, <code>state</code> must be exactly <code>on</code> or <code>off</code> and selects the Consume state deterministically. The <code>repeatMode</code> action selects its mode deterministically: <code>off</code> means MPD <code>repeat off</code>/<code>single off</code>, <code>all</code> means <code>repeat on</code>/<code>single off</code>, and <code>one</code> means <code>repeat on</code>/<code>single on</code>. The legacy <code>repeat</code> toggle remains unchanged; <code>repeat-mode</code> is accepted as a compatibility spelling of <code>repeatMode</code>.</p><p>Success responses contain only the normalized action and safe state:</p><pre><code>{&quot;ok&quot;:true,&quot;action&quot;:&quot;seekRelative&quot;,&quot;seconds&quot;:-30}
+{&quot;ok&quot;:true,&quot;action&quot;:&quot;repeat&quot;,&quot;repeatOn&quot;:true}
+{&quot;ok&quot;:true,&quot;action&quot;:&quot;consume&quot;,&quot;state&quot;:&quot;on&quot;,&quot;consumeOn&quot;:true}
+{&quot;ok&quot;:true,&quot;action&quot;:&quot;repeatMode&quot;,&quot;mode&quot;:&quot;one&quot;,&quot;repeatMode&quot;:&quot;one&quot;,&quot;repeatOn&quot;:true,&quot;singleOn&quot;:true}</code></pre><p><code>consume</code> responses always include authoritative MPD readback as both normalized <code>state</code> and boolean <code>consumeOn</code>, including for the blind-toggle form. Invalid states return <code>400</code> <code>MOBILE_CONSUME_STATE_INVALID_REQUEST</code>; unavailable or mismatched authoritative readback returns <code>502</code> <code>MOBILE_CONSUME_READBACK_MISMATCH</code>. <code>repeatMode</code> responses include the authoritative MPD readback as <code>mode</code> and <code>repeatMode</code>, plus <code>repeatOn</code> and <code>singleOn</code>. Invalid modes return <code>400</code> <code>MOBILE_REPEAT_MODE_INVALID_REQUEST</code>; a failed authoritative readback returns <code>502</code> <code>MOBILE_REPEAT_MODE_READBACK_MISMATCH</code>.</p><p>The route delegates to the existing guarded playback authority, so it does not release or take over Qobuz Connect or Sonuvi UPnP. If Qobuz owns playback, the response for any Home mutation, including Consume, is <code>409</code> with <code>code: &quot;HOME_PLAYBACK_OWNERSHIP_CONFLICT&quot;</code> and <code>source: &quot;qobuz-connect&quot;</code>; the client must call the explicit <code>POST /v1/mobile/qobuz-connect/release</code> contract first and retry after confirmed release. Sonuvi UPnP ownership returns the same <code>409</code> code with <code>source: &quot;upnp-sonuvi&quot;</code>; the client must use the UPnP mobile contract instead. Existing web/controller Track-Key routes remain unchanged.</p><h2 id="current-track-controls">Current-track controls</h2><p>The native client may read and change favorite/rating state through the same bearer-authenticated current-track contract. The mobile read is derived from the canonical <code>/now-playing</code> snapshot, including its <code>isFavorite</code>, <code>rating</code>, and <code>ratingDisabled</code> fields. Mutations resolve the file from that same canonical snapshot (falling back to the current mobile queue item only when the snapshot has no file), then pass it server-side to the existing file-aware favorite/rating routes. The client never submits an MPD file path or rating-file path.</p><pre><code>POST /v1/mobile/now-playing/favorite
 Authorization: Bearer &lt;mobile-session-token&gt;
 Content-Type: application/json
 
@@ -56,9 +74,28 @@ Authorization: Bearer &lt;mobile-session-token&gt;</code></pre><p>The server inv
 Authorization: Bearer &lt;mobile-session-token&gt;
 
 GET /v1/mobile/audio-info
-Authorization: Bearer &lt;mobile-session-token&gt;</code></pre><p><code>/v1/mobile/next-up</code> returns the web controller&#x27;s target-aware successor. The iPad presents it as <strong>Next Playing</strong> and opens the shared Live Queue when tapped. The shelf also shows the current queue position/length from the now-playing payload or the durable device queue.</p><p>When Live Queue is selected as a Home discovery row, the native remote poll also reads the complete upcoming queue and reconciles that row. This covers queue additions made by another controller; local playback uses the device queue directly.</p><p><code>/v1/mobile/audio-info</code> invokes the existing Track-Key-protected moOde reader server-side and returns only sanitized <code>section</code>, <code>key</code>, and <code>value</code> rows plus an optional fetch timestamp. It never exposes the moOde source URL, Track Key, MPD file path, or raw admin response to the native client.</p><h2 id="local-audio-sourcecache">Local audio source/cache</h2><p>The server catalog and queue remain authoritative when the native client plays to This iPhone/iPad. The attached SSD and Application Support store are byte sources below the normal canonical <code>MobileTrack</code> flow; they are not additional catalogs, queues, playlists, or track identities.</p><p>The client obtains an authenticated availability manifest:</p><pre><code>GET /v1/mobile/local-source/manifest
+Authorization: Bearer &lt;mobile-session-token&gt;</code></pre><p><code>/v1/mobile/next-up</code> returns the web controller&#x27;s target-aware successor. The iPad presents it as <strong>Next Playing</strong> and opens the shared Live Queue when tapped. The shelf also shows the current queue position/length from the now-playing payload or the durable device queue.</p><p>When Live Queue is selected as a Home discovery row, the native remote poll also reads the complete upcoming queue and reconciles that row. This covers queue additions made by another controller; local playback uses the device queue directly.</p><p><code>/v1/mobile/audio-info</code> invokes the existing Track-Key-protected moOde reader server-side and returns sanitized <code>section</code>, <code>key</code>, and <code>value</code> rows plus an optional fetch timestamp. Source/file-path rows are reduced to a source class such as <code>Local file</code> or <code>Network stream</code>; the response never exposes the moOde source URL, Track Key, MPD file path, raw admin response, or moOde PHP session identifier to the native client.</p><p>When this route is opened, the Server requests the optional on-demand diagnostics view from the Track-Key route. The response may include the backward-compatible, versioned <code>diagnostics</code> object:</p><pre><code>{
+  &quot;version&quot;: &quot;audio-info.v1&quot;,
